{"content_hash": "b0428482d998476c", "version_id": 603, "article_id": 61, "level_code": "en_b1", "lang": "en", "level": 3, "level_label": "B1 中级", "title": "Mum warns parents: don't miss the signs of child cancer", "title_zh": "妈妈提醒家长：别忽视儿童癌症的信号", "has_translation": true, "topic": "health", "source": "bbc_health", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwm237z77dgyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1616408621507-88179d36a3a0?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8Mnx8Y2hpbGRob29kJTIwY2FuY2VyJTIwc3ltcHRvbXN8ZW58MHwwfHx8MTc5MTc2NTkwNnww&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Vitolda Klein on Unsplash", "author": "Vitolda Klein", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@little_klein?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/girl-in-white-and-blue-floral-dress-lying-on-white-fur-textile-ebiDpT4pYdw?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(195, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(233, 46%, 48%)", "label": "HEALTH", "seed": "2c5b225ed52f"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-12", "word_count": 211, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "Childhood cancer is rare, but it is the biggest disease killer of children in the UK. One mother shares her story because many parents do not feel sure about the warning signs.", "lead_zh": "儿童癌症很少见，但它是英国儿童因病死亡的头号原因。一位母亲分享了自己的经历，因为很多家长对警示信号并不确定。", "preview": "About 1,900 children in the UK are diagnosed with cancer every year. However, just under a third of parents say they feel confident about re", "preview_zh": "英国每年约有1900名儿童被诊断出癌症。然而，只有不到三分之一的家长表示，他们自信能认出这些症状。13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出白血病。她的妈妈杰玛说，最初的迹象很容易被忽略。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 593, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/61/en_b1.mp3", "duration": 82.987, "size_bytes": 501696, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural", "word_count": 242, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@aa22245/content/data/versions/603.json", "paragraphs": ["About 1,900 children in the UK are diagnosed with cancer every year. However, just under a third of parents say they feel confident about recognising the symptoms. Amelia Carroll, who is 13, was diagnosed with leukaemia last year. Her mother Gemma says the first signs were easy to miss.", "Amelia felt tired and was out of breath after light exercise. Although these problems seemed normal, she later got bruises that could not be explained. Within ten days, doctors told her she had acute myeloid leukaemia. She is now in remission and has gone back to school.", "Professor Bob Phillips is a children's cancer doctor. He says parents should look for groups of symptoms that continue, get worse, or get bigger. These include extreme tiredness, pale skin, strange bruises, and lumps that do not go away. If a child has three symptoms, or sees a doctor three times, or has three weeks of problems, a referral may be needed.", "Survival rates have improved a lot since the 1970s. Now eight out of ten children live for five to ten years or more after diagnosis. Phillips says quicker diagnosis would mean less difficult treatment. About half of children get a diagnosis within two weeks, but he wants the other half to wait less."], "paragraphs_zh": ["英国每年约有1900名儿童被诊断出癌症。然而，只有不到三分之一的家长表示，他们自信能认出这些症状。13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出白血病。她的妈妈杰玛说，最初的迹象很容易被忽略。", "阿米莉亚感到疲倦，稍微运动后就喘不上气。虽然这些问题看起来很正常，但她后来出现了无法解释的淤青。十天内，医生告诉她，她得了急性髓系白血病。她现在病情缓解，已经回到学校。", "鲍勃·菲利普斯教授是一名儿童癌症医生。他说，家长应该留意那些持续存在、越来越严重或越来越大的症状组合。这些包括极度疲倦、脸色苍白、不明原因的淤青，以及不消退的肿块。如果孩子有三种症状，或看了三次医生，或有三周的问题，可能需要转诊。", "自20世纪70年代以来，存活率已大幅提高。现在，十个孩子中有八个在诊断后能活五年到十年甚至更久。菲利普斯说，更快的诊断意味着更轻松的治疗。约一半的孩子在两周内得到诊断，但他希望另一半不用等那么久。"], "body": "About 1,900 children in the UK are diagnosed with cancer every year. However, just under a third of parents say they feel confident about recognising the symptoms. Amelia Carroll, who is 13, was diagnosed with leukaemia last year. Her mother Gemma says the first signs were easy to miss.\n\nAmelia felt tired and was out of breath after light exercise. Although these problems seemed normal, she later got bruises that could not be explained. Within ten days, doctors told her she had acute myeloid leukaemia. She is now in remission and has gone back to school.\n\nProfessor Bob Phillips is a children's cancer doctor. He says parents should look for groups of symptoms that continue, get worse, or get bigger. These include extreme tiredness, pale skin, strange bruises, and lumps that do not go away. If a child has three symptoms, or sees a doctor three times, or has three weeks of problems, a referral may be needed.\n\nSurvival rates have improved a lot since the 1970s. Now eight out of ten children live for five to ten years or more after diagnosis. Phillips says quicker diagnosis would mean less difficult treatment. About half of children get a diagnosis within two weeks, but he wants the other half to wait less.", "body_zh": "英国每年约有1900名儿童被诊断出癌症。然而，只有不到三分之一的家长表示，他们自信能认出这些症状。13岁的阿米莉亚·卡罗尔去年被诊断出白血病。她的妈妈杰玛说，最初的迹象很容易被忽略。\n\n阿米莉亚感到疲倦，稍微运动后就喘不上气。虽然这些问题看起来很正常，但她后来出现了无法解释的淤青。十天内，医生告诉她，她得了急性髓系白血病。她现在病情缓解，已经回到学校。\n\n鲍勃·菲利普斯教授是一名儿童癌症医生。他说，家长应该留意那些持续存在、越来越严重或越来越大的症状组合。这些包括极度疲倦、脸色苍白、不明原因的淤青，以及不消退的肿块。如果孩子有三种症状，或看了三次医生，或有三周的问题，可能需要转诊。\n\n自20世纪70年代以来，存活率已大幅提高。现在，十个孩子中有八个在诊断后能活五年到十年甚至更久。菲利普斯说，更快的诊断意味着更轻松的治疗。约一半的孩子在两周内得到诊断，但他希望另一半不用等那么久。", "text": "About 1,900 children in the UK are diagnosed with cancer every year. However, just under a third of parents say they feel confident about recognising the symptoms. Amelia Carroll, who is 13, was diagnosed with leukaemia last year. Her mother Gemma says the first signs were easy to miss. Amelia felt tired and was out of breath after light exercise. Although these problems seemed normal, she later got bruises that could not be explained. Within ten days, doctors told her she had acute myeloid leukaemia. She is now in remission and has gone back to school. Professor Bob Phillips is a children's cancer doctor. He says parents should look for groups of symptoms that continue, get worse, or get bigger. These include extreme tiredness, pale skin, strange bruises, and lumps that do not go away. If a child has three symptoms, or sees a doctor three times, or has three weeks of problems, a referral may be needed. Survival rates have improved a lot since the 1970s. Now eight out of ten children live for five to ten years or more after diagnosis. Phillips says quicker diagnosis would mean less difficult treatment. About half of children get a diagnosis within two weeks, but he wants the other half to wait less.", "vocab": [{"word": "diagnose", "pos": "verb", "zh": "诊断", "note": "常用被动语态：be diagnosed with...", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "symptom", "pos": "noun", "zh": "症状", "note": "可数名词，常搭配 show/recognise symptoms", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "confident", "pos": "adj", "zh": "自信的", "note": "feel confident about doing sth", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "recognise", "pos": "verb", "zh": "认出，识别", "note": "英式拼写，美式为 recognize", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "bruise", "pos": "noun", "zh": "淤青", "note": "也可作动词，表示“碰伤”", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "leukaemia", "pos": "noun", "zh": "白血病", "note": "英式拼写，美式为 leukemia", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "remission", "pos": "noun", "zh": "缓解期", "note": "常搭配 in remission，指病情暂时好转", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "survival rate", "pos": "phrase", "zh": "存活率", "note": "常用复数 survival rates", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "referral", "pos": "noun", "zh": "转诊", "note": "make a referral 表示“转诊”", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "lump", "pos": "noun", "zh": "肿块", "note": "可数名词，常与 get bigger 搭配", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for", "summary_original": "Although rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK.", "body_original": "Childhood cancer symptoms can be easily missed - here's what to look out for\nBy\nCamilla Horrox\nHealth reporter\nPublished\n1 hour ago\nAlthough rare, cancer is the leading cause of death from disease among children under 15 in the UK - yet just under a third of parents say they feel confident recognising the symptoms.\nAbout 1,900 children aged 0-14 are diagnosed with cancer each year, according to Cancer Research UK.\nThirteen-year-old Amelia Carroll, who is now in remission, was diagnosed with acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) last year.\nHer mum Gemma recalls the initial symptoms being easy to overlook.\nThere was the tiredness, she says, and being out of breath after mild exercise but both were put down to being unfit and just post-school fatigue.\nIt was only when Amelia started presenting with bruises Gemma knew something wasn't right - within 10 days Amelia got her leukaemia diagnosis.\nWhat are the symptoms to look out for?\nProf Bob Phillips, a paediatric oncologist, says it's key for parents to look out for clusters of symptoms that may be persisting, getting worse or bigger.\nConsider contacting a doctor over the following:\nExtreme tiredness\nLooking really pale\nBruises that you can't explain\nLumps and bumps that are getting bigger or not going away and growing over time\nCancer in children develops much more quickly than in adults - effectively due to them growing.\nExperts like Phillips at CCLG, the Children and Young People's Cancer Association, are urging people to follow a national campaign aiming to raise awareness and improve diagnosis times.\nIt includes a rule-of-three for professionals: If a child has had three persistent symptoms, or has visited their doctor three times, or has had three weeks of unexplained symptoms consider making a referral.\nAlthough, it's important to note survival rates have risen significantly since the 1970s, with eight out of 10 children surviving beyond five to 10 years or more after diagnosis.\nFor Amelia, her cancer journey began with a single red bruise that came out of nowhere, says Gemma.\nThree days later, she had bruises down both of her shins on both legs, which were sore and raised.\n\"We'd gone to the opticians for a routine appointment and they noticed that she'd got some burst blood vessels but they weren't sure why,\" she adds.\nWithin 10 days, doctors had confirmed what type of leukaemia Amelia had and she was admitted to hospital to begin treatment.\n\"On the day that they confirmed what she had, I sat her down and she said to me 'have I got cancer?' and I said yes you have. She asked me if she was going to die.\"\n\"I said no, they're doing everything they can, they've got a plan in place and the treatment is going to do everything it can to beat the cancer.\"\nAmelia is currently in remission, undergoing regular bone marrow and blood tests every three months, and has returned to school.\nPhillips says survival rates for childhood cancer in the UK are over 80% but that even quicker diagnosis would lead to less intensive treatments.\n'Raising awareness is his legacy'\nAround the UK, about half the children diagnosed with cancer get their diagnosis within two weeks, he says.\n\"It's pretty quick but we want the other half to not have the wait of 16, 18, 20, 25 weeks from those first symptoms to get a diagnosis.\"\nCancer Research UK data shows that there are more than 400,000 new cases of cancer diagnosed every year in the UK, with only 54% being told within the 28-day NHS target window, according to the British Medical Journal (BMJ).\nAn additional 2,300 teenagers and young adults (aged 15–24) are diagnosed each year.\nLynsey Swinscoe is mum to Jake, who died of soft tissue cancer aged 13. Jake was diagnosed with stage three, fusion positive alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma in July 2023.\nBefore his diagnosis, Jake's initial symptoms of a stuffy nose and watery eyes were put down to hayfever, and falling asleep after school was thought to be just end-of-term tiredness.\n\"It was only when he started to get a swelling across the bridge of his nose that we thought right, we need to take him to the GP,\" Lynsey says.\n\"But at that point we didn't really think the fatigue and hayfever symptoms were related.\"\nA mass was found in Jake's nose and nasal cavity and he was diagnosed with soft tissue sarcoma. He completed nine rounds of chemotherapy and proton beam radiation.\nAfter suffering seizures and feeling very unwell from the chemotherapy, Jake opted out of continuing treatment.\nHe passed away peacefully at home on 26 April 2024, just two weeks before his 14th birthday.\n\"I think the main thing with Jake is he was not scared to die. We had lots of conversations about dying and what it might feel like to die and the only thing that he was sort of scared about was, 'are all the people going to remember me? Is everyone just going to forget who I am?'\n\"That's why I'm doing this, to make sure that people know who Jake is, to make sure people know how incredible he was. And so with that comes the raising awareness, which I think, that's his legacy.\"\nRelated topics\nLeukaemia\nCancer\nChild health\nNHS\nHealth\nChildhood cancer\nMore on this story\nMother writes book to help children with cancer\nPublished\n4 October\nGirl, 7, rings bell to mark end of cancer treatment\nPublished\n1 October\n'This doesn't happen to us' - mum on child's cancer\nPublished\n12 September", "fetched_at": "2026-10-12T00:45:05.386127", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "About", "s": 0.1, "e": 0.438, "sm": 100, "em": 437, "cs": 0, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "1,900", "s": 0.45, "e": 1.725, "sm": 450, "em": 1724, "cs": 6, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "children", "s": 1.738, "e": 2.138, "sm": 1737, "em": 2137, "cs": 12, "ce": 20, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "in", "s": 2.15, "e": 2.225, "sm": 2150, "em": 2225, "cs": 21, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "the", "s": 2.237, "e": 2.287, "sm": 2237, "em": 2287